            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Mica Mineralogy: Sheet Silicate Crystal Structure & Dielectric Supremacy</h3>\n            <p><strong>Mica</strong> is a group of phyllosilicate (sheet silicate) minerals characterized by a layered atomic architecture of tetrahedral silica sheets loosely linked by interlayer potassium or sodium cations. This unique crystallographic structure imparts <strong>perfect basal cleavage</strong> in one plane, allowing mica crystals ('books') to be cleaved cleanly into remarkably thin, flexible, transparent sheets measuring less than one-hundredth of a millimeter in thickness!</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Commercial Species of Mica</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Muscovite (Potash Mica / White Mica - $KAl_2(AlSi_3O_{10})(OH)_2$):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Colorless, silver-white, light green, or ruby-red.</li>\n                        <li>Possesses supreme dielectric strength, chemical inertness, and thermal stability up to 500°C–600°C. Known commercially as <strong>Ruby Mica</strong> (when tinted reddish-brown) or <strong>White Mica</strong>. The primary commercial variety mined in India.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Phlogopite (Magnesium Mica / Amber Mica - $KMg_3(AlSi_3O_{10})(OH)_2$):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Yellowish-brown to amber; exhibits higher thermal stability than muscovite, withstanding continuous temperatures up to <strong>900°C–1,000°C</strong> without decomposing. Mined in Kerala (Kollam) and Andhra Pradesh (Visakhapatnam).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Biotite (Black Mica / Iron-Magnesium Mica - $K(Mg,Fe)_3(AlSi_3O_{10})(OH)_2$):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Dark green, brown to jet black. High iron content degrades its electrical insulation capacity, rendering it commercially unsuitable for high-grade electrical sheets.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Why Mica is Indispensable in Electrical & Electronics Engineering</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Supreme Dielectric Strength:</strong> Withstands intense electrical voltage gradients (thousands of volts per millimeter) without electrical breakdown or puncture.</li>\n                <li><strong>Negligible Power Loss Factor (Low Dissipation):</strong> Minimizes energy dissipation as waste heat during high-frequency alternating currents, making it the premier dielectric in precision <strong>capacitors, radar transmitters, radio frequency tubes, and aerospace avionics</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Incombustibility & Thermal Endurance:</strong> Completely non-flammable; used in toaster heating elements, electric iron heating plates, and spark plug insulation.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
