            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>The Bihar-Jharkhand Mica Belt: Koderma & Bengal Ruby Mica</h3>\n            <p>For more than a century, an elongated east-west trending geological strip spanning northern Jharkhand and southern Bihar was recognized across the globe as the <strong>premier source of sheet mica on Earth</strong>. At its zenith, this belt supplied over <strong>75% to 80% of the entire world's demand for high-grade electrical sheet mica</strong>!</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Geographical Extent & Geological Setting</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Belt:</strong> Extends over a length of approximately <strong>150 to 160 kilometers</strong> with a width of 20 to 30 kilometers, stretching across <strong>Koderma, Giridih, and Hazaribagh districts in Jharkhand</strong>, and extending northwards into <strong>Nawada and Gaya districts in Bihar</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Host Rocks:</strong> Thousands of coarse-grained pegmatite veins intrude Archean mica schists and gneisses of the Chota Nagpur Granite Gneiss Complex (CGC).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Koderma: The 'Mica Capital of the World'</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Koderma Town:</strong> Universally christened as the <em>'Mica Capital of the World'</em> (or <em>Abhrak Nagari</em>). Koderma served as the commercial nerve center for the mining, sorting, splitting, and global export of sheet mica to the US, Europe, and Japan.</li>\n                <li><strong>Bengal Ruby Mica:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The muscovite produced from Koderma, Domchanch, Tisri, and Giridih exhibits an exquisite ruby or pale brownish-red tint.</li>\n                        <li>Known in international commodity markets as <strong>'Bengal Ruby Mica'</strong> (named during the British Raj when the territory fell under the Bengal Presidency).</li>\n                        <li>Unsurpassed in physical perfection, freedom from mineral stains, structural flatness, and dielectric puncture resistance.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Decline of Deep Mining & Shift to 'Dhibra' Collection</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The enactment of the <strong>Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980</strong> designated large tracts of the Koderma mica forests as reserved forests and wildlife sanctuaries (Koderma Wildlife Sanctuary), leading to the non-renewal of formal underground mining leases.</li>\n                <li>Consequently, deep shaft mining ceased, transitioning the local economy into the informal scavenging of historic mine dump tailings—locally termed <strong>'Dhibra' (mica scrap and flakes)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
