            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Andhra Pradesh & Rajasthan Mica Belts: Nellore & The Aravalli Pegmatites</h3>\n            <p>While the Bihar-Jharkhand belt was historically renowned for ruby mica sheets, modern commercial mica extraction in India has shifted geographically to the coastal pegmatite belt of <strong>Andhra Pradesh</strong> and the Aravalli fold belt of <strong>Rajasthan</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Nellore Mica Belt (Andhra Pradesh): India’s Current Production Leader</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Setting:</strong> An arcuate belt stretching over <strong>100 kilometers</strong> with a width of 25 km in the <strong>SPSR Nellore district</strong> of southern coastal Andhra Pradesh.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Mining Centers:</strong> <strong>Gudur, Rapur, Saidapuram, Atmakur, and Kavali</strong>. Gudur serves as the primary commercial processing, cutting, and export trading hub.</li>\n                <li><strong>Characteristic Mineral Quality:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Nellore muscovite is globally distinguished by its distinctive <strong>light-green to yellowish-green tint</strong> (hence known as <em>'Nellore Green Mica'</em>), in contrast to the ruby tint of Koderma.</li>\n                        <li>Possesses high structural flatness, flexibility, and excellent electrical insulating qualities.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Current Standing:</strong> Andhra Pradesh is currently the <strong>largest producer of crude and scrap mica in India</strong>, accounting for the vast majority of official commercial output.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Rajasthan Mica Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Spread:</strong> Extends across an extensive 320-km northeast-southwest trending zone from <strong>Jaipur through Tonk, Ajmer, and Bhilwara to Udaipur</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Bhilwara District: The Nerve Center:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Bhilwara is the primary mica-producing district in Rajasthan, centered around mining tracts in <strong>Mandal, Bhilwara, Jahazpur, and Asind</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Rajasthan mica is predominantly ruby-colored or pale green, occurring in large pegmatites cutting Precambrian Aravalli schists.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Specialized Industry:</strong> Bhilwara developed an indigenous industry manufacturing <strong>insulating mica bricks</strong> for lining steel blast furnaces and glass kilns.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
