            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Limestone in India: Reserves, Production Rankings & Major Cement Corridors</h3>\n            <p>India is the <strong>second largest cement producer in the world</strong> (after China), boasting a total national cement production capacity exceeding <strong>600 million tonnes per annum</strong>. Because raw limestone is a heavy, low-value, weight-losing bulk raw material (requiring approximately 1.4 to 1.5 tonnes of limestone to produce 1 tonne of cement clinker), India’s giant cement manufacturing plants are strictly tied to localized limestone quarry clusters.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Leading States in Limestone RESERVES</h4>\n            <p>According to the National Mineral Inventory (IBM / Ministry of Mines), the descending order of Indian states in total in-situ limestone reserves is:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Karnataka (~27%–28%) &gt; Andhra Pradesh (~20%) &gt; Rajasthan (~12%) &gt; Gujarat (~10%) &gt; Meghalaya (~9%)\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Karnataka:</strong> Holds over one-fourth of India's total limestone resources, concentrated in the Proterozoic <strong>Bhima and Kaladgi basins</strong> across Kalaburagi (Gulbarga), Bagalkot, and Belagavi districts.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Andhra Pradesh:</strong> The massive <strong>Cuddapah (Kadapa) and Palnadu basins</strong> (Narji limestone).</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Meghalaya:</strong> The southern slopes of the Khasi, Jaintia, and Garo Hills host pristine, high-grade Tertiary Eocene limestones (Shella Formation).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Leading States in Limestone PRODUCTION</h4>\n            <p>In terms of annual commercial extraction volume, the ranking reflects active industrial utilization:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Rajasthan (~21%–23%) &gt; Madhya Pradesh (~13%–14%) &gt; Andhra Pradesh (~12%–13%) &gt; Chhattisgarh (~11%) &gt; Karnataka (~9%–10%)\n            </div>\n\n            <h4>3. Major Limestone & Cement Clusters of India</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Rajasthan:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Chittorgarh ('The Cement Capital of India'):</strong> Dense concentration of mega-plants (Nimbahera, Chanderiya, Shambhupura).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Lakheri (Bundi district):</strong> Historic site of <strong>India's first commercial Portland cement plant</strong>, commissioned in 1914 by the Associated Cement Companies (ACC).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Sanu (Jaisalmer district):</strong> Famous for high-purity SMS and chemical-grade chalky limestone mined by RSMML.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Madhya Pradesh (Satna-Rewa-Maihar Belt):</strong> The Kaimur-Bhander limestone corridor along the Vindhyan escarpment, hosting India’s largest cluster of pit-head cement plants.</li>\n                <li><strong>Karnataka (Wadi-Sedam Belt in Kalaburagi):</strong> The cement powerhouse of South India, feeding Maharashtra and Karnataka urban markets.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chhattisgarh (Durg-Raipur-Baloda Bazar Corridor):</strong> High-grade Hirri and Chandi limestones feeding plants at Jamul, Hirmi, and Mandhar.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
