            "title": "Gypsum: The Rajasthan Monopoly, Plaster of Paris & Agricultural Soil Reclamation",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Gypsum: Chemistry, Rajasthan’s Absolute Monopoly & Soil Reclamation</h3>\n            <p><strong>Gypsum</strong> is a soft, naturally occurring hydrated sulfate mineral chemically formulated as <strong>Calcium Sulfate Dihydrate ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$)</strong>. It defines hardness <strong>2 on Mohs Scale of Mineral Hardness</strong> (easily scratched by a human fingernail). Gypsum exhibits extreme spatial concentration in India, with the desert state of <strong>Rajasthan holding a near-total national monopoly in both reserves and production</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Rajasthan’s Absolute National Monopoly</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Reserves:</strong> Rajasthan holds <strong>over 81% to 82% of India's total in-situ gypsum reserves</strong> (~1,050 million tonnes of resources). Minor deposits occur in Jammu & Kashmir (Baramulla), Gujarat, and Tamil Nadu.</li>\n                <li><strong>Production:</strong> Rajasthan accounts for <strong>over 99% of India's total annual mined commercial gypsum production!</strong></li>\n                <li><strong>Premier Deposits & Mines:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Jamsar (Bikaner District):</strong> Discovered in the early 20th century; recognized as <strong>India’s largest single gypsum deposit</strong>. Mined by public sector enterprise <em>Rajasthan State Mines and Minerals Limited (RSMML)</em>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Nagaur District:</strong> The subterranean gypsum beds of <strong>Bhadwasi, Manglod, and Pilanwasi</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Barmer & Jaisalmer Districts:</strong> Extensive desert surface bedded deposits (Kawad, Mohangarh).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Primary Industrial & Agricultural Applications</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. The Indispensable Cement Retarder (>50% of Demand):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>When Portland cement clinker is ground in ball mills, <strong>2% to 3% gypsum is ground into the cement powder</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Crucial Role:</strong> Gypsum acts as a chemical set retarder. Without gypsum, the tricalcium aluminate ($C_3A$) in cement hydrates violently within seconds of adding water (<em>'flash set'</em>), preventing construction masons from working or placing the concrete!</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Plaster of Paris (POP) & Drywall Construction:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>When raw gypsum is calcined at <strong>120°C to 150°C</strong>, it loses three-fourths of its crystal water, forming <strong>Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 0.5H_2O$)</strong>, universally known as <strong>Plaster of Paris (POP)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Recombines with water into a rigid, fire-resistant crystalline matrix used for wall plaster, gypsum ceiling drywall, orthopaedic bone fracture casts, and dental molds.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Agricultural Reclamation of Alkaline / Sodic Soils:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Gypsum is the primary chemical amendment used to reclaim unproductive alkaline (Usar / Kallar) soils across Punjab, Haryana, and Uttar Pradesh.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Chemical Mechanism:</strong> The soluble calcium ions ($Ca^{2+}$) from gypsum displace toxic exchangeable sodium ions ($Na^+$) from the soil clay complex, which are then leached away by irrigation water, restoring soil fertility and structure.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Gypsum is Calcium Sulfate Dihydrate ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$), defining Hardness 2 on Mohs scale.",
                "Rajasthan holds >81% of India's gypsum reserves and produces >99% of national gypsum output.",
                "Jamsar in Bikaner district is India's largest single gypsum deposit, operated by RSMML.",
                "Essential cement retarder: 2%–3% gypsum is ground into cement to prevent instantaneous flash setting.",
                "Calcining gypsum at 120°C–150°C yields Plaster of Paris (POP - $CaSO_4 \\cdot 0.5H_2O$).",
                "Vital agricultural soil conditioner for reclaiming alkaline/sodic soils in the Indo-Gangetic plains."
            ],
