            "title": "Strategic Nuclear Minerals: Uranium Mineralogy & The Singhbhum Shear Zone",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Strategic Nuclear Minerals: Uranium Mineralogy & The Singhbhum Cradle</h3>\n            <p><strong>Uranium ($U$)</strong> is a dense, silvery-white radioactive metallic element belonging to the actinide series ($Z = 92$). With its fissile isotope <strong>Uranium-235 ($^{235}U$)</strong> capable of sustaining controlled nuclear fission chain reactions, uranium is the foundational fuel for nuclear power reactors and strategic nuclear deterrence. In India, uranium exploration and production are protected under statutory state monopoly.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Uranium Minerals & Occurrence</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Uraninite / Pitchblende ($U_3O_8$):</strong> Uranium oxide. A heavy, pitch-black to dark brown radioactive mineral; the principal commercial ore of uranium worldwide and in India.</li>\n                <li><strong>Coffinite ($U(SiO_4)_{1-x}(OH)_{4x}$):</strong> Hydrated uranium silicate; prominent in hydrothermal vein deposits.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Singhbhum Shear Zone (Jharkhand): The Cradle of Indian Uranium</h4>\n            <p>For more than half a century, the <strong>Singhbhum Shear Zone (Copper Belt Thrust)</strong> in East Singhbhum district, Jharkhand, has served as the operational backbone of India's indigenous nuclear energy program, operated exclusively by the public sector enterprise <strong>Uranium Corporation of India Limited (UCIL)</strong> (under the Department of Atomic Energy - DAE):</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Jaduguda Mine (Commissioned 1967):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>India’s First Commercial Uranium Mine:</strong> Sinks to a vertical depth of nearly <strong>880 to 900 meters</strong> via a high-capacity vertical production shaft. Houses India’s oldest uranium ore processing mill.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Narwapahar Mine:</strong> Commissioned in 1995; acclaimed as <strong>India’s first fully mechanized trackless underground uranium mine</strong>, utilizing modern decline ramps and electro-hydraulic drill jumbos.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Bhatin, Turamdih, Mohuldih & Bagjata:</strong> Major underground producing mines along the thrust zone.</li>\n                <li><strong>4. Banduhurang Mine:</strong> Commissioned in 2007; <strong>India’s first and only commercial open-cast uranium mine</strong>, extracting low-grade uranium ore.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Hydrometallurgical Extraction & 'Yellowcake'</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Crushed uranium ore is leached with dilute Sulfuric Acid ($H_2SO_4$) in the presence of pyrolusite ($MnO_2$) oxidant, ion-exchanged, and precipitated with magnesia or ammonia into a dry, concentrated uranium peroxide powder known universally as <strong>'Yellowcake' (Magnesium Diuranate / Ammonium Diuranate)</strong>.</li>\n                <li>Yellowcake is securely transported to the <strong>Nuclear Fuel Complex (NFC) in Hyderabad</strong>, where it is refined into pure nuclear-grade Uranium Dioxide ($UO_2$) fuel pellets for India's Pressurized Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
