            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Thorium Mineralogy: The Fertile Giant in India’s Coastal Placer Sands</h3>\n            <p><strong>Thorium ($Th$)</strong> is a naturally occurring radioactive metallic element ($Z = 90$). While natural uranium contains only 0.7% fissile material, <strong>natural thorium is 100% composed of the isotope Thorium-232 ($^{232}Th$)</strong>. Thorium-232 is not fissile itself, but it is <strong>fertile</strong>: when irradiated by neutrons inside a nuclear reactor, it absorbs a neutron and transmutes via beta decay into <strong>Uranium-233 ($^{233}U$)</strong>, an extraordinary fissile nuclear fuel that releases colossal atomic energy upon fission.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. India's Incomparable Global Thorium Endowment</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>While India possesses less than 1% to 2% of the world’s uranium reserves, the nation holds <strong>over 25% of the entire world's known geological resources of Thorium</strong>!</li>\n                <li>According to the Atomic Minerals Directorate (AMD), India possesses over <strong>12.7 million tonnes of Monazite</strong>, containing approximately <strong>1.07 million tonnes of Thorium Oxide ($ThO_2$)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Mineralogy: Monazite Heavy Mineral Sands</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Monazite ($Ce,La,Nd,Th(PO_4)$):</strong> A dense, reddish-brown monoclinic phosphate mineral with a specific gravity of 5.0 to 5.2.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chemical Assay:</strong> Monazite contains approximately <strong>8% to 10.5% Thorium Dioxide ($ThO_2$)</strong>, 0.3% to 0.4% Uranium Oxide ($U_3O_8$), and roughly <strong>60% Rare Earth Oxides (REO)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Coastal Beach Placer Belts of India</h4>\n            <p>Heavy mineral sands (placer deposits) are concentrated along Indian coastlines through natural wave refraction, longshore drift, and hydraulic sorting:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Kerala Coast:</strong> The world-famous <strong>Chavara-Neendakara placer deposit (Kollam district)</strong>. Mined by <em>Kerala Minerals and Metals Limited (KMML)</em> and <em>IREL</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Tamil Nadu Coast:</strong> <strong>Manavalakurichi (Kanyakumari district)</strong>, Kudankulam, and Tirunelveli-Tuticorin beach dunes.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Odisha Coast:</strong> <strong>Chhatrapur (Ganjam district)</strong>, site of IREL’s massive <em>OSCOM (Odisha Sands Complex)</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>4. Andhra Pradesh Coast:</strong> Extensive heavy mineral sand placers in Srikakulam (Bhavanapadu) and Visakhapatnam (Bheemunipatnam).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
