            "id": "m15",
            "title": "Other Critical Industrial Minerals: Barytes (Mangampet Giant), Wollastonite & Magnesite",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Critical Industrial Minerals: Barytes, Wollastonite, Magnesite & Glass Sand</h3>\n            <p>Beyond bulk construction commodities and nuclear elements, several specialized industrial non-metallic minerals exhibit extreme geographic concentration in India, commanding powerful domestic monopolies and leading global export positions.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Barytes (Barite - $BaSO_4$): The Mangampet Phenomenon</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Mineral Characteristics:</strong> Barium Sulfate ($BaSO_4$). Known for its extraordinary density for a non-metallic mineral, boasting a <strong>high specific gravity of 4.5</strong>. It is chemically inert and non-magnetic.</li>\n                <li><strong>Industrial Hegemony (>85% of Demand):</strong> Used as a heavy weighting agent in <strong>oil and gas well drilling muds</strong>. The dense barium mud exerts hydrostatic counter-pressure down the drill hole, preventing high-pressure gas pockets from blowing out the drilling rig ('blowout prevention').</li>\n                <li><strong>The Mangampet Giant (YSR Kadapa / Cuddapah District, Andhra Pradesh):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Operated by the <em>Andhra Pradesh Mineral Development Corporation (APMDC)</em>.</li>\n                        <li>Recognized as the <strong>single largest bedded deposit of barytes in the entire world</strong>! Contains over 94% of India’s barytes reserves.</li>\n                        <li>Andhra Pradesh accounts for <strong>over 95% of India's total barytes production</strong>, making India one of the world's top two exporters of drilling-grade barytes (to the Gulf, US, and North Sea oilfields).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Wollastonite ($CaSiO_3$): Rajasthan’s Absolute Monopoly</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Mineralogy:</strong> Calcium Inosilicate ($CaSiO_3$). Known for its naturally acicular (needle-shaped) fiber morphology.</li>\n                <li><strong>Applications:</strong> A non-hazardous, safe substitute for asbestos; enhances thermal shock resistance in ceramic wall tiles and high-friction automotive brake linings.</li>\n                <li><strong>Rajasthan Monopoly:</strong> Rajasthan holds <strong>over 95% of India’s reserves and accounts for 100% of national production</strong> (mining centered in <strong>Sirohi, Udaipur, and Dungarpur districts</strong>). India ranks among the top 3 global producers!</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Magnesite ($MgCO_3$): Refractory Metallurgy</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Chalk Hills (Salem District, Tamil Nadu):</strong> Renowned for pure white cryptocrystalline magnesite forming a network of veins in dunite rocks. Historically supplied basic refractory bricks to steel plants.</li>\n                <li><strong>Uttarakhand Belts:</strong> Crystalline bedded magnesite in Almora (Jhiroli) and Pithoragarh (Chandak).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Silica Sand / Glass Sand</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>High-purity quartz sand ($SiO_2 \\ge 99\\%$) used in glassmaking. The <strong>Shankargarh-Bargarh belt (Prayagraj, UP / Chitrakoot, MP)</strong> is India’s premier glass sand corridor.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
